Z||000007||88formula||05-09-2001||04:52 PM||||An engine needs three things to run, air, fuel, and spark. I'll assume you have air and spark so that leaves you with fuel. I would recommend that you start with the easy stuff first and through the process of elimination trace the fuel problem to its source. An old school easy way to see if fuel is all the engines needs to run is to sprinkle some fuel into the TBI unit and crank the engine. Don't just assume it’s the fuel pump or varnish and drop the tank. I've had cars that sat longer than that and start up. First get a cheap haynes manual and find the fuel pump relay. Check for voltage and ground. It may have rusted contacts and they may be stuck open. Check your fuses and fuel pump fuse. On some GM cars if the oil pressure switch is faulty the power to the fuel pump will be cut off. There is a fuel pump test terminal, I can't remember where it is but the manual will tell you or someone here can. Your best bet is to get the manual, they are usually about $15.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Nikotek||05-09-2001||07:22 PM||||Many thanks for all the welcomes and the help,I will post pics of my 355 kit,88'coupe and my 308 "Magnum"(which may be for sale soon). In reply to 88formula,I do in fact have spark and air-and did drop some fuel in the tbi.It started and quit after that.I have 2 relays mounted next to each other,one has power(left one)and is reported to be the fuel pump relay.The other has no current(which should be the oil pressure relay)yet I have power at the plug on the oil pressure sender itself.My gauge shows no pressure when key is turned...I was under the impression the oil pressure sending unit was a backup if the pump relay fails.Does anyone know if both relays should be hot,I have no fusible links that are out.Wondering if I can supply 12v to the aldl terminal to test it and whether any ground is needed doing a test like this?&gt;<BR>Thanks again everyone,post pics soon for anyone interested.<P>------------------<BR>87' GT (355 Spyder)<BR>88' Coupe||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||05-09-2001||08:17 PM||||First. Put a test light in ALDL terminal G and ground the other end. If the relay is bad or the pump is otherwise not getting power then you'll see that here. The pump relay should energize this line for a few seconds after you turn the key on.<P><BR>There are notes to aid in proper terminal ID in my cave. (Link below...)<P>There is very little in this circuit.<P>make sure the fuses are all good. Pull it out and physically inspect it if need be.<P>If you hear the relay then the ECM is likely fine. After your sure of the fuses your down to the relay or the pump dead.<P>Yes, you can apply 12V to terminal G to kick over the pump. Be Certain you are using terminal G and that you don't short to any other terminal or you could fry the ECM. You do not have to worry about ground.<P><B>WARNING!!!!</B><BR>You MUST use a fused power feed for this test! the fuse MUST be the same or less Amp Rating than the one normally running the pump!<P>Failure to use a fused feed could cause the pump to fry. Can you say "Center Fuel Tank Explosion"? As in TWA-800....<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Nikotek||05-10-2001||12:25 AM||||Lots of good places to start and thanks for the "heads-up" theogre,I would'nt have thought of adding an inline fuse before testing--could have been messy!I'll do some tests on it tomorrow,what's the standard way of posting pics here...the usual "&lt;IMG&gt;pic &lt;/IMG&gt;"?<BR><P>------------------<BR>87' GT (355 Spyder)<BR>88' Coupe||||reg||1
